// Phase 5: Backends. What makes an API survive real users. Clocks are passed in so that time-based logic is testable.

unit({ id: 'auth', phase: 5, title: 'Passwords and sessions', mins: 11, read: [
`<p class="lede">Assume your database will be stolen one day. Plan for it. <b>The goal: even with every row in hand, the attacker still can't use your users' passwords.</b></p>`,
steps([
  { n: 'Storing passwords as they are: one leak, and every account is gone. Most people reuse passwords, so their other accounts go too.', d: D.grid([['user', 'password'], ['sam', 'hunter2'], ['ira', 'hunter2']], { x: 40, y: 10, s: 90, hl: { '1,1': 'r', '2,1': 'r' } }) },
  { n: 'A hash is one-way: easy to compute, practically impossible to reverse. To check a login, hash what was typed and compare. But two users with the same password get the same hash, so attackers precompute hashes of common passwords.', d: D.grid([['user', 'hash'], ['sam', '7f3a'], ['ira', '7f3a']], { x: 40, y: 10, s: 90, hl: { '1,1': 'y', '2,1': 'y' } }) + D.t(340, 80, 'same hash: a giveaway', { cls: 'r' }) },
  { n: 'A salt is random and unique per user, mixed in before hashing. Same password, different hash. Precomputed tables become useless. The salt isn\'t secret, and it sits right beside the hash.', d: D.grid([['user', 'salt', 'hash'], ['sam', 'x9', 'c21e'], ['ira', 'k2', '88ab']], { x: 40, y: 10, s: 90, hl: { '1,2': 'g', '2,2': 'g' } }) },
  { n: 'Stretch it: hash thousands of times, so each guess costs the attacker real time. A login takes 100 ms, which you won\'t notice. A billion guesses take years. Real code uses bcrypt or argon2, which do all this for you.', d: D.flow({ a: [30, 40, 170, 60, 'password + salt'], b: [260, 30, 180, 80, 'hash, hash, hash,\n...100,000 times', 'y'], c: [510, 40, 170, 60, 'slow to attack', 'g'] }, [['a', 'b'], ['b', 'c']]) }
], 170),
`<h2>Sessions: proving who you are, on every request</h2><p>HTTP forgets you between requests. So after login, the server hands you a token, and you send it on every later request.</p>`,
fig(D.flow({ c: [20, 50, 130, 60, 'client'], s: [290, 50, 130, 60, 'server'], d: [560, 50, 140, 60, 'token store', 'y'] }, [['s', 'd', 'checks']]) + D.arrow(150, 66, 290, 66, 'b') + D.t(220, 56, 'password', { a: 'middle', cls: 'b', size: 14 }) + D.arrow(290, 94, 150, 94, 'g') + D.t(220, 112, 'token', { a: 'middle', cls: 'g', size: 14 }), 130),
`<h2>Rules you don't get to skip</h2><ul>
<li><b>Tokens must be random from a secure source:</b> Python's <code>secrets</code>, never <code>random</code>, whose output can be predicted.</li>
<li><b>Compare secrets in constant time:</b> <code>hmac.compare_digest</code>. An ordinary <code>==</code> stops at the first different character, and tiny timing differences have leaked real secrets.</li>
<li><b>Same message for "no such user" and "wrong password".</b> Otherwise, you tell attackers which emails exist.</li>
<li><b>Tokens expire.</b> And logout must really delete them.</li></ul>
<h2>Signed tokens</h2><p>Instead of storing tokens, the server can sign them: <code>data + HMAC(secret, data)</code>. Anyone can read the data, but nobody without the secret can alter it. This is the idea behind JWT. It needs no lookups, but you also can't easily revoke one before it expires.</p>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [30, 30, 240, 50, 'user=7;exp=1700', 'y'], b: [270, 30, 150, 50, 'signature', 'g'] }) + D.t(30, 110, 'change one letter of the data, and the signature no longer matches.', { cls: 'r' }), 125),
think('Why not encrypt passwords instead of hashing them?', 'Encryption can be reversed with a key, and the key is sitting near the data. If the attacker gets both, every password falls at once. A hash has nothing to steal. You never need the original back.')
], core: [
{ id: 'password-hash', title: 'Store passwords properly', diff: 'Medium', time: 15,
  desc: `<p><code>hash_password(pw)</code> returns a single string containing a random salt and the stretched hash. <code>check_password(pw, stored)</code> returns True if it matches. Use <code>hashlib.sha256</code> in a loop of at least 1,000 rounds. A real app would use bcrypt or argon2 instead: this is to see how they work. Use <code>secrets</code> for the salt and <code>hmac.compare_digest</code> for comparing.</p>`,
  ex: [['check_password("hunter2", hash_password("hunter2"))', 'True']], start: 'import hashlib, hmac, secrets\n\ndef hash_password(pw):\n    pass\n\ndef check_password(pw, stored):\n    pass\n',
  nudge: ['How will check_password find the salt? It has to be stored somewhere.', 'Write a helper used by both, so the rounds can\'t differ.'],
  sol: `import hashlib, hmac, secrets\n\nROUNDS = 2000\n\ndef _stretch(pw, salt):\n    h = (salt + pw).encode()\n    for _ in range(ROUNDS):\n        h = hashlib.sha256(h).digest()\n    return h.hex()\n\ndef hash_password(pw):\n    salt = secrets.token_hex(16)\n    return salt + "$" + _stretch(pw, salt)\n\ndef check_password(pw, stored):\n    salt, _, digest = stored.partition("$")\n    return hmac.compare_digest(_stretch(pw, salt), digest)`, big: 'Deliberately slow: rounds × hash cost', idea: 'A per-user salt, a slow hash, and a constant-time comparison.',
  tests: [['check_password("hunter2", hash_password("hunter2"))', 'True'], ['check_password("wrong", hash_password("hunter2"))', 'False'], ['hash_password("a") != hash_password("a")', 'True'], ['"hunter2" not in hash_password("hunter2")', 'True'], ['check_password("", hash_password(""))', 'True'], ['check_password("नमस्ते", hash_password("नमस्ते"))', 'True'],
    ['elapsed > base * 100', 'True', 'import time, hashlib\nt = time.perf_counter()\nhash_password("x")\nelapsed = time.perf_counter() - t\nt = time.perf_counter()\nhashlib.sha256(b"x" * 40).digest()\nbase = time.perf_counter() - t\nbase = max(base, 1e-7)']] },
{ id: 'signed-token', title: 'Tamper-proof tokens', diff: 'Medium', time: 16,
  desc: `<p><code>make_token(user_id, expires_at, secret)</code> returns a string. <code>read_token(token, secret, now)</code> returns the <code>user_id</code> (an int) if the signature is right and <code>now &lt; expires_at</code>, otherwise <code>None</code>. Use <code>hmac</code> with SHA-256. A garbage token must return None and not crash.</p>`,
  ex: [['read_token(make_token(7, 100, "k"), "k", 50)', '7'], ['read_token(make_token(7, 100, "k"), "k", 100)', 'None']], start: 'import hmac, hashlib\n\ndef make_token(user_id, expires_at, secret):\n    pass\n\ndef read_token(token, secret, now):\n    pass\n',
  nudge: ['What exactly gets signed? What if an attacker changes expires_at?', 'How do you split the token safely if it\'s garbage?'],
  sol: `import hmac, hashlib\n\ndef _sign(data, secret):\n    return hmac.new(secret.encode(), data.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n\ndef make_token(user_id, expires_at, secret):\n    data = "%d.%d" % (user_id, expires_at)\n    return data + "." + _sign(data, secret)\n\ndef read_token(token, secret, now):\n    parts = token.split(".") if isinstance(token, str) else []\n    if len(parts) != 3:\n        return None\n    data = parts[0] + "." + parts[1]\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(_sign(data, secret), parts[2]):\n        return None\n    if now >= int(parts[1]):\n        return None\n    return int(parts[0])`, big: 'O(token length)', idea: 'Sign everything that matters. Check the signature before you trust anything.',
  tests: [['read_token(make_token(7, 100, "k"), "k", 50)', '7'], ['read_token(make_token(7, 100, "k"), "k", 100)', 'None'], ['read_token(make_token(7, 100, "k"), "other", 50)', 'None'], ['read_token("garbage", "k", 1)', 'None'], ['read_token("", "k", 1)', 'None'], ['read_token("a.b.c", "k", 1)', 'None'], ['read_token(None, "k", 1)', 'None'],
    ['read_token(forged, "k", 50)', 'None', 't = make_token(7, 100, "k")\nparts = t.split(".")\nforged = t.replace(str(7), str(8), 1)'], ['read_token(t2, "k", 500)', 'None', 't = make_token(7, 100, "k")\nt2 = t.replace("100", "999", 1)']] }
], boss:
{ id: 'auth-service', title: 'Login service', diff: 'Hard', time: 28,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>Auth(clock)</code> where <code>clock()</code> returns the current time in seconds.</p><ul><li><code>register(email, password)</code>: <code>False</code> if the email exists (case-insensitive) or the password is under 8 characters, otherwise <code>True</code>.</li><li><code>login(email, password)</code>: returns a random token string on success, otherwise <code>None</code>. Tokens last 3600 seconds.</li><li><code>whoami(token)</code>: returns the email as first registered, or <code>None</code> if unknown or expired (exactly 3600 seconds later counts as expired).</li><li><code>logout(token)</code>: the token stops working.</li></ul><p><b>Lockout:</b> after 5 failed logins in a row for an email, further logins for that email fail for 300 seconds, even with the right password. A success resets the count. Passwords must never be stored in plain form.</p>`,
  ex: [['a.register("s@x", "password1"); t = a.login("s@x", "password1"); a.whoami(t)', '"s@x"']], start: 'import hashlib, secrets\n\nclass Auth:\n    ', nudge: [],
  sol: `import hashlib, hmac, secrets\n\nclass Auth:\n    def __init__(self, clock):\n        self.clock = clock\n        self.users = {}\n        self.tokens = {}\n        self.fails = {}\n\n    def _hash(self, pw, salt):\n        h = (salt + pw).encode()\n        for _ in range(1000):\n            h = hashlib.sha256(h).digest()\n        return h.hex()\n\n    def register(self, email, password):\n        key = email.lower()\n        if key in self.users or len(password) < 8:\n            return False\n        salt = secrets.token_hex(8)\n        self.users[key] = {"email": email, "salt": salt, "hash": self._hash(password, salt)}\n        return True\n\n    def login(self, email, password):\n        key = email.lower()\n        now = self.clock()\n        count, locked_until = self.fails.get(key, (0, 0))\n        if now < locked_until:\n            return None\n        u = self.users.get(key)\n        if not u or not hmac.compare_digest(u["hash"], self._hash(password, u["salt"])):\n            count += 1\n            self.fails[key] = (0, now + 300) if count >= 5 else (count, 0)\n            return None\n        self.fails.pop(key, None)\n        token = secrets.token_urlsafe(32)\n        self.tokens[token] = (u["email"], now + 3600)\n        return token\n\n    def whoami(self, token):\n        entry = self.tokens.get(token)\n        if not entry:\n            return None\n        email, expires = entry\n        if self.clock() >= expires:\n            del self.tokens[token]\n            return None\n        return email\n\n    def logout(self, token):\n        self.tokens.pop(token, None)`, big: 'O(1) per call, apart from the deliberate hash cost', idea: 'State per email and per token. Pass the clock in, so that time can be tested.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'now = [0]\na = Auth(lambda: now[0])\na.register("s@x", "password1")\n'; const fail5 = 'for _ in range(5):\n    a.login("s@x", "wrong")\n'; return [['a.whoami(a.login("s@x", "password1"))', '"s@x"', s], ['a.login("s@x", "nope")', 'None', s], ['a.login("who@x", "password1")', 'None', s], ['a.register("S@X", "password2")', 'False', s], ['a.register("b@x", "short")', 'False', s], ['a.whoami(t)', 'None', s + 't = a.login("s@x", "password1")\na.logout(t)\n'], ['a.whoami(t)', '"s@x"', s + 't = a.login("s@x", "password1")\nnow[0] = 3599\n'], ['a.whoami(t)', 'None', s + 't = a.login("s@x", "password1")\nnow[0] = 3600\n'], ['a.whoami("nope")', 'None', s],
    ['a.login("s@x", "password1")', 'None', s + fail5], ['a.login("s@x", "password1") is not None', 'True', s + fail5 + 'now[0] = 300\n'], ['a.login("s@x", "password1") is not None', 'True', s + 'for _ in range(4):\n    a.login("s@x", "wrong")\n'], ['a.login("s@x", "password1") is not None', 'True', s + 'for _ in range(4):\n    a.login("s@x", "wrong")\na.login("s@x", "password1")\nfor _ in range(4):\n    a.login("s@x", "wrong")\n'],
    ['"password1" in repr(a.__dict__)', 'False', s], ['a.login("s@x", "password1") != a.login("s@x", "password1")', 'True', s]]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'middleware', phase: 5, title: 'Middleware and permissions', mins: 7, read: [
`<p class="lede">Every request needs logging, auth, error handling and so on. Copying those lines into every handler is how bugs get in. <b>Middleware wraps the handler like layers of an onion.</b></p>`,
fig(`<rect class="mk mk-y" x="40" y="14" width="640" height="150" rx="8"/><rect class="mk mk-b" x="110" y="40" width="500" height="100" rx="8"/><rect class="mk mk-g" x="200" y="62" width="320" height="56" rx="8"/>` + D.t(360, 32, 'logging', { a: 'middle' }) + D.t(360, 58, 'auth', { a: 'middle' }) + D.t(360, 95, 'your handler', { a: 'middle' }) + D.arrow(10, 70, 200, 70, 'r') + D.t(50, 62, 'request', { cls: 'r', size: 14 }) + D.arrow(520, 110, 710, 110, 'g') + D.t(630, 104, 'response', { cls: 'g', size: 14 }), 180, 'A layer can act on the way in, act on the way out, or stop the request and answer right there.'),
`<h2>In code: a function that takes a function</h2>`,
`<pre><code>def logged(next_handler):
    def wrapper(request):
        print("in:", request["path"])
        response = next_handler(request)   # go one layer deeper
        print("out:", response[0])
        return response
    return wrapper                         # looks just like a handler</code></pre>`,
`<p>Because a wrapped handler looks the same as a handler, you can stack as many as you like.</p>
<h2>401 or 403?</h2>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [20, 40, 170, 60, 'Who are you?\n(authentication)', 'y'], b: [270, 40, 170, 60, 'May you do this?\n(authorization)', 'b'], c: [520, 40, 170, 60, 'your handler', 'g'] }, [['a', 'b', 'yes'], ['b', 'c', 'yes']]) + D.t(105, 130, 'no: 401', { a: 'middle', cls: 'r' }) + D.t(355, 130, 'no: 403', { a: 'middle', cls: 'r' }), 145),
`<h2>The most common real-world security bug</h2><p>A user requests <code>/invoices/42</code>. They're logged in. Is invoice 42 <b>theirs</b>? Checking "is logged in" and forgetting "owns this" lets anyone read any record by changing a number. It's called broken object-level authorization, and it tops the charts of API vulnerabilities year after year.</p>`,
think('Order matters: should logging go outside auth, or inside?', 'Outside. You want a record of the rejected requests too, because that is how you notice an attack.')
], core: [
{ id: 'mw-chain', title: 'Stack the layers', diff: 'Medium', time: 12,
  desc: `<p><code>chain(handler, *layers)</code> returns a new handler. Each layer is a function taking the next handler and returning a handler. The <b>first</b> layer listed must be the <b>outermost</b>. With no layers, it returns the handler itself.</p>`,
  ex: [['chain(h, a, b)', 'a runs first, then b, then h']], start: 'def chain(handler, *layers):\n    ',
  nudge: ['To end up with a on the outside, which layer do you wrap first?'],
  sol: `def chain(handler, *layers):\n    for layer in reversed(layers):\n        handler = layer(handler)\n    return handler`, big: 'O(layers)', idea: 'Wrap from the inside out.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'log = []\ndef mk(name):\n    def layer(nxt):\n        def w(r):\n            log.append(name + " in")\n            out = nxt(r)\n            log.append(name + " out")\n            return out\n        return w\n    return layer\ndef h(r):\n    log.append("h")\n    return r + 1\n'; return [['(chain(h, mk("a"), mk("b"))(1), log)', '(2, ["a in", "b in", "h", "b out", "a out"])', s], ['chain(h)(1)', '2', s], ['chain(h, stop)(1)', '"blocked"', s + 'def stop(nxt):\n    return lambda r: "blocked"\n']]; })() },
{ id: 'mw-errors', title: 'Never crash the server', diff: 'Easy', time: 10,
  desc: `<p><code>catch_errors(next)</code> is a layer. If the next handler returns normally, pass its result through. If it raises <code>ValueError</code>, return <code>(400, {"error": str(e)})</code>. If it raises anything else, call <code>report(e)</code> and return <code>(500, {"error": "internal"})</code>, with no internal details in the response. <code>report</code> is a global function supplied by the app.</p>`,
  ex: [['handler raises ValueError("bad")', '(400, {"error": "bad"})']], start: 'def catch_errors(next):\n    ',
  nudge: ['Why must internal error details never reach the client?'],
  sol: `def catch_errors(next):\n    def wrapper(request):\n        try:\n            return next(request)\n        except ValueError as e:\n            return (400, {"error": str(e)})\n        except Exception as e:\n            report(e)\n            return (500, {"error": "internal"})\n    return wrapper`, big: 'O(1) added', idea: 'One place turns exceptions into responses. Clients get a clear answer, and you get the details.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'seen = []\nreport = lambda e: seen.append(str(e))\ndef ok(r):\n    return (200, r)\ndef bad(r):\n    raise ValueError("bad input")\ndef boom(r):\n    raise KeyError("db password is hunter2")\n'; return [['catch_errors(ok)("x")', '(200, "x")', s], ['catch_errors(bad)("x")', '(400, {"error": "bad input"})', s], ['catch_errors(boom)("x")', '(500, {"error": "internal"})', s], ['(catch_errors(boom)("x"), len(seen))[1]', '1', s], ['"hunter2" in str(catch_errors(boom)("x"))', 'False', s]]; })() }
], boss:
{ id: 'mw-access', title: 'Whose invoice is it?', diff: 'Medium', time: 20,
  desc: `<p>A request is a dict: <code>{"token": ..., "params": {...}}</code>. Write two layers.</p><ul><li><code>authenticate(sessions)(next)</code>: looks up <code>request.get("token")</code> in dict <code>sessions</code> (token to user dict). If absent, return <code>(401, {"error": "login required"})</code>. Otherwise call next with a <b>new</b> request that has a <code>"user"</code> key added. The caller's dict must not change.</li><li><code>owner_only(invoices)(next)</code>: looks up <code>request["params"]["id"]</code> in dict <code>invoices</code> (id to invoice dict with <code>"owner"</code>). If missing, <code>404</code>. If <code>invoice["owner"] != user["id"]</code> and <code>user.get("role") != "admin"</code>, return <code>404</code>, not 403, so that outsiders can't tell which ids exist. Otherwise call next.</li></ul>`,
  ex: [['no token', '(401, ...)'], ["someone else's invoice", '(404, ...)']], start: 'def authenticate(sessions):\n    pass\n\ndef owner_only(invoices):\n    pass\n', nudge: [],
  sol: `def authenticate(sessions):\n    def layer(next):\n        def wrapper(request):\n            user = sessions.get(request.get("token"))\n            if user is None:\n                return (401, {"error": "login required"})\n            return next({**request, "user": user})\n        return wrapper\n    return layer\n\ndef owner_only(invoices):\n    def layer(next):\n        def wrapper(request):\n            inv = invoices.get(request["params"]["id"])\n            user = request["user"]\n            if inv is None or (inv["owner"] != user["id"] and user.get("role") != "admin"):\n                return (404, {"error": "not found"})\n            return next(request)\n        return wrapper\n    return layer`, big: 'O(1)', idea: 'Being logged in is not the same as owning this. 404 also avoids confirming that an id exists.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'S = {"t1": {"id": 1}, "t2": {"id": 2}, "ad": {"id": 9, "role": "admin"}}\nI = {7: {"owner": 1}}\nh = lambda r: (200, r["user"]["id"])\napp = authenticate(S)(owner_only(I)(h))\n'; const c = (tok, id) => `app({"token": ${tok}, "params": {"id": ${id}}})`; return [[c('"t1"', 7), '(200, 1)', s], [`${c('"t9"', 7)}[0]`, '401', s], ['app({"params": {"id": 7}})[0]', '401', s], [`${c('"t2"', 7)}[0]`, '404', s], [`${c('"t1"', 8)}[0]`, '404', s], [c('"ad"', 7), '(200, 9)', s], ['"user" in req', 'False', s + 'req = {"token": "t1", "params": {"id": 7}}\napp(req)\n']]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'pagination-indexes', phase: 5, title: 'Pagination and indexes', mins: 10, read: [
`<p class="lede">Your API works on 100 rows. At 10 million it dies. Two ideas get you most of the way: <b>never return everything, and never make the database read everything.</b></p>`,
`<h2>What an index is</h2>`,
steps([
  { n: 'With no index, finding one email means reading every row: a full scan. It\'s O(n), and it gets slower every day your app succeeds.', d: D.cells(['zed', 'amy', 'kim', 'sam', 'bob', 'ira'], { x: 120, y: 30, w: 70, hl: { 0: 'r', 1: 'r', 2: 'r', 3: 'g' } }) + D.t(120, 110, 'reading row by row, looking for sam', { cls: 'r' }) },
  { n: 'An index is a separate sorted structure, like the index at the back of a book: value to row location. It\'s a B-tree, a wide cousin of the BST. Lookups cost O(log n), which is about 4 reads for a billion rows.', d: D.flow({ a: [270, 6, 180, 36, 'a-k | l-z', 'y'], b: [120, 66, 180, 36, 'a-e | f-k'], c: [420, 66, 180, 36, 'l-r | s-z', 'g'], d: [420, 126, 180, 36, 'sam: row 3', 'g'] }, [['a', 'b'], ['a', 'c', '', 'g'], ['c', 'd', '', 'g']]) }
], 175),
`<h2>Nothing is free</h2><p>Each index makes reads faster, and every write slower, because the index has to be updated as well. Add them for queries you actually run. Use <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> to see what the database will do: "SCAN" means reading everything, "SEARCH ... USING INDEX" is what you want.</p>
<h2>Column order in composite indexes</h2><p>An index on <code>(city, age)</code> is sorted like a phone book: by city, then by age within a city. So it helps <code>WHERE city = ?</code>, and <code>WHERE city = ? AND age &gt; ?</code>. It can't help <code>WHERE age &gt; ?</code> by itself, just as a phone book can't find everyone with a given first name.</p>
<h2>Pagination: offset vs cursor</h2>`,
steps([
  { n: '<code>LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000000</code>: the database must walk past a million rows, and throw them away, on every request. Deep pages get slower and slower.', d: D.cells(['', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', ''], { x: 40, y: 30, w: 46, hl: { 0: 'r', 1: 'r', 2: 'r', 3: 'r', 4: 'r', 5: 'r', 6: 'g', 7: 'g' } }) + D.t(40, 110, 'all that red was read and discarded', { cls: 'r' }) },
  { n: 'And if a row is inserted while someone is paging, everything shifts by one. The next page shows a row they\'ve already seen, or misses one.', d: D.cells(['new', '1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8'], { x: 40, y: 30, w: 46, hl: { 0: 'y', 4: 'r' } }) + D.t(40, 110, 'row 3 appears on two pages', { cls: 'r' }) },
  { n: '<b>Cursor:</b> "give me 20 rows after id 1000". The index jumps straight there. It costs the same at any depth, and inserts don\'t shift anything. You hand the client a cursor, and they send it back.', d: D.cells(['', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', ''], { x: 40, y: 30, w: 46, hl: { 6: 'g', 7: 'g' }, ptr: { 'WHERE id > ?': 6 } }) }
], 150),
think('Sorting by <code>created_at</code>, two rows can share a timestamp. What goes wrong with a cursor on <code>created_at</code> alone, and how do you fix it?', 'Rows with the same timestamp can be skipped or repeated at a page boundary. Sort by <code>(created_at, id)</code>, and make the cursor both values. The id breaks the ties.')
], core: [
{ id: 'cursor-page', title: 'Cursor pagination', diff: 'Medium', time: 14,
  desc: `<p>Table <code>posts(id, title)</code>. <code>page(db, limit, cursor=None)</code> returns <code>{"items": [titles], "next": c}</code>, in id order. <code>next</code> is the value to pass to get the following page, or <code>None</code> if nothing's left. Don't use OFFSET. Don't make an extra query for whether there's more: one query can tell you.</p>`,
  ex: [['3 posts, limit 2', '{"items": ["a", "b"], "next": 2}']], start: 'def page(db, limit, cursor=None):\n    ',
  nudge: ['How can a single query tell you if another row exists after this page?'],
  sol: `def page(db, limit, cursor=None):\n    rows = db.execute("SELECT id, title FROM posts WHERE id > ? ORDER BY id LIMIT ?", (cursor or 0, limit + 1)).fetchall()\n    has_more = len(rows) > limit\n    rows = rows[:limit]\n    return {"items": [r[1] for r in rows], "next": rows[-1][0] if has_more else None}`, big: 'O(log n + limit)', idea: 'Ask for one row more than you need: its presence is the answer.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE posts (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT)")\ndb.executemany("INSERT INTO posts (title) VALUES (?)", [("a",), ("b",), ("c",)])\nqs = []\ndb.set_trace_callback(qs.append)\n'; return [['page(db, 2)', '{"items": ["a", "b"], "next": 2}', s], ['page(db, 2, 2)', '{"items": ["c"], "next": None}', s], ['page(db, 3)', '{"items": ["a", "b", "c"], "next": None}', s], ['page(db, 2, 9)', '{"items": [], "next": None}', s], ['(page(db, 2), any("OFFSET" in q.upper() for q in qs), len(qs))[1:]', '(False, 1)', s], ['page(db, 2, nx)', '{"items": ["d"], "next": None}', s + 'nx = page(db, 2)["next"]\ndb.execute("DELETE FROM posts WHERE id = 1")\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO posts (title) VALUES (\'d\')")\ndb.execute("DELETE FROM posts WHERE id = 3")\n']]; })() },
{ id: 'add-index', title: 'Stop the scan', diff: 'Medium', time: 10,
  desc: `<p>This table serves one hot query: <code>SELECT * FROM events WHERE user_id = ? AND kind = ? ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 20</code>. <code>index_sql()</code> should return a <code>CREATE INDEX</code> statement that lets the database use the index for the filter <b>and</b> avoid a separate sort. Columns: <code>events(id, user_id, kind, ts, data)</code>.</p>`,
  ex: [['', 'EXPLAIN shows SEARCH USING INDEX, and no TEMP B-TREE']], start: 'def index_sql():\n    return """\n    \n    """\n',
  nudge: ['Think of a phone book. Which columns must come first so you can jump straight to the right place?', 'If the sorted column comes after the filter columns, is the data already in the order you want?'],
  sol: `def index_sql():\n    return "CREATE INDEX idx_events_hot ON events (user_id, kind, ts)"`, big: 'O(log n + 20)', idea: 'Equality columns first, then the sort column.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE events (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, user_id INT, kind TEXT, ts INT, data TEXT)")\ntry:\n    db.execute(index_sql())\nexcept sqlite3.Error:\n    pass\nplan = " ".join(str(r[3]) for r in db.execute("EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT * FROM events WHERE user_id = 1 AND kind = \'a\' ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 20"))\n'; return [['"USING INDEX" in plan', 'True', s], ['"TEMP B-TREE" in plan', 'False', s], ['"user_id=?" in plan and "kind=?" in plan', 'True', s]]; })() }
], boss:
{ id: 'n-plus-one', title: 'The N+1 problem', diff: 'Medium', time: 16,
  desc: `<p>The starter works, but it makes 1 query for the posts, and another for each post. 100 posts means 101 database round trips. Rewrite so it makes <b>at most 2</b> queries in total. Return the same result: a list of <code>{"title": ..., "author": ...}</code> in post id order. <code>author</code> is <code>None</code> if the user no longer exists.</p>`,
  ex: [['', '[{"title": "a", "author": "sam"}, ...]']],
  start: `def feed(db):\n    out = []\n    for id, title, author_id in db.execute("SELECT id, title, author_id FROM posts ORDER BY id").fetchall():\n        row = db.execute("SELECT name FROM users WHERE id = ?", (author_id,)).fetchone()\n        out.append({"title": title, "author": row[0] if row else None})\n    return out\n`, nudge: [],
  sol: `def feed(db):\n    q = """SELECT p.title, u.name FROM posts p LEFT JOIN users u ON u.id = p.author_id ORDER BY p.id"""\n    return [{"title": t, "author": a} for t, a in db.execute(q).fetchall()]`, big: 'One query', idea: 'Round trips cost far more than work in the database. Fetch together.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.executescript("""CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE posts (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT, author_id INT);\nINSERT INTO users VALUES (1,\'sam\'),(2,\'ira\');\nINSERT INTO posts VALUES (1,\'a\',2),(2,\'b\',1),(3,\'c\',9);""")\nqs = []\ndb.set_trace_callback(qs.append)\n'; return [['feed(db)', '[{"title": "a", "author": "ira"}, {"title": "b", "author": "sam"}, {"title": "c", "author": None}]', s], ['(feed(db), len(qs) <= 2)[1]', 'True', s]]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'transactions', phase: 5, title: 'Transactions', mins: 10, read: [
`<p class="lede">Moving money means subtracting from one account and adding to another. <b>If the server dies between those two steps, money vanishes.</b> Transactions exist to make sure that can never happen.</p>`,
steps([
  { n: 'Two separate writes. A crash in the middle, and 50 is just gone.', d: D.flow({ a: [40, 30, 160, 60, 'sam: 100 → 50', 'y'], b: [480, 30, 160, 60, 'ira: 0'] }) + D.t(340, 66, 'crash here', { a: 'middle', cls: 'r', size: 22 }) + D.t(40, 125, 'money in the system: was 100, now 50', { cls: 'r' }) },
  { n: 'A transaction: all the steps happen, or none do. On a crash, the database undoes the partial work. That\'s atomicity.', d: D.box(30, 16, 620, 90, 'b') + D.flow({ a: [50, 30, 160, 60, 'sam − 50'], b: [470, 30, 160, 60, 'ira + 50'] }) + D.t(340, 66, 'all or nothing', { a: 'middle', cls: 'g', size: 20 }) }
], 145),
`<table><tr><th></th><th>ACID means</th></tr><tr><td><b>Atomic</b></td><td>All or nothing.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Consistent</b></td><td>Rules (constraints) hold before and after.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Isolated</b></td><td>Concurrent transactions don't see each other's half-done work.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Durable</b></td><td>Once it says committed, it survives a power cut.</td></tr></table>
<h2>Two people, one seat</h2>`,
steps([
  { n: 'A and B both read: seats left = 1.', d: D.flow({ a: [40, 20, 120, 50, 'A reads 1'], b: [40, 90, 120, 50, 'B reads 1'], d: [460, 50, 170, 60, 'seats = 1', 'y'] }) },
  { n: 'Both think "1 is enough". Both write 0, and both get a confirmation. One seat, two passengers. It\'s called a lost update, and it happens constantly in naive code.', d: D.flow({ a: [40, 20, 120, 50, 'A writes 0', 'r'], b: [40, 90, 120, 50, 'B writes 0', 'r'], d: [460, 50, 170, 60, 'seats = 0', 'r'] }) },
  { n: 'Fix 1: make the check and the change one single statement. <code>UPDATE seats SET n = n - 1 WHERE id = ? AND n > 0</code>, then look at how many rows changed. Zero means you lost the race, safely.', d: D.flow({ a: [120, 40, 470, 60, 'UPDATE ... WHERE n > 0  (one atomic step)', 'g'] }) },
  { n: 'Fix 2, optimistic locking: add a version column. Update only "where version = what I read". If someone got there first, zero rows change, and you retry. Good when conflicts are rare.', d: D.flow({ a: [70, 40, 570, 60, 'UPDATE ... SET version = 4 WHERE id = ? AND version = 3', 'g'] }) }
], 150),
`<h2>Keep them short</h2><p>A transaction holds locks. Don't call another service or wait on anything slow while one's open, or everyone else queues behind you. And a remote call can't be rolled back: if you charged a card, a database rollback doesn't un-charge it.</p>`,
think('You read the balance in Python, check it, then write the new balance, all inside one transaction. Is it safe against a concurrent transfer?', 'It depends on the isolation level, so often not. Safer is one conditional UPDATE: <code>SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ? AND balance >= ?</code>, then check rowcount.')
], core: [
{ id: 'tx-transfer', title: 'Move the money', diff: 'Medium', time: 15,
  desc: `<p>Table <code>accounts(id, balance)</code>, with a rule that balance can't go below zero. <code>transfer(db, a, b, amount)</code> moves money and returns True. Return False and change <b>nothing</b> if: amount ≤ 0, <code>a</code> equals <code>b</code>, either account is missing, or funds are short. Both changes must happen in one transaction. Test databases are set up to fail on the second step sometimes: after that, balances must be unchanged and the exception must not escape.</p>`,
  ex: [['transfer(db, 1, 2, 30)', 'True']], start: 'def transfer(db, a, b, amount):\n    ',
  nudge: ['Can a single statement check the balance and subtract in one step?', 'How do you tell a missing destination? What should happen to the debit you already made?'],
  sol: `import sqlite3\n\ndef transfer(db, a, b, amount):\n    if amount <= 0 or a == b:\n        return False\n    try:\n        with db:\n            if db.execute("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ? AND balance >= ?", (amount, a, amount)).rowcount != 1:\n                raise ValueError("cannot debit")\n            if db.execute("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?", (amount, b)).rowcount != 1:\n                raise ValueError("cannot credit")\n        return True\n    except (ValueError, sqlite3.Error):\n        return False`, big: 'O(1) with a primary key', idea: 'Raising inside the transaction block rolls everything back.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE accounts (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, balance INT NOT NULL)")\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (1, 100), (2, 0)")\ndb.commit()\nbal = lambda: [r[0] for r in db.execute("SELECT balance FROM accounts ORDER BY id")]\n'; return [['(transfer(db, 1, 2, 30), bal())', '(True, [70, 30])', s], ['(transfer(db, 1, 2, 500), bal())', '(False, [100, 0])', s], ['(transfer(db, 1, 2, -5), bal())', '(False, [100, 0])', s], ['(transfer(db, 1, 2, 0), bal())', '(False, [100, 0])', s], ['(transfer(db, 1, 1, 5), bal())', '(False, [100, 0])', s], ['(transfer(db, 1, 9, 5), bal())', '(False, [100, 0])', s], ['(transfer(db, 9, 1, 5), bal())', '(False, [100, 0])', s], ['(transfer(db, 1, 2, 100), bal())', '(True, [0, 100])', s],
    ['(transfer(db, 1, 2, 5), bal())', '(False, [100, 0])', s + 'db.execute("CREATE TRIGGER no_ira BEFORE UPDATE ON accounts WHEN NEW.id = 2 BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, \'bank is down\'); END")\ndb.commit()\n'], ['(db.rollback(), bal())[1]', '[70, 30]', s + 'transfer(db, 1, 2, 30)\n']]; })() }
], boss:
{ id: 'tx-seats', title: 'Last seat on the bus', diff: 'Hard', time: 22,
  desc: `<p>Tables <code>buses(id, seats, version)</code> and <code>bookings(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, bus_id, user)</code>. Write:</p><ul><li><code>book(db, bus, user)</code>: books a seat and returns True, or returns False when there are no seats left, the bus doesn't exist, or the user already has a booking on this bus. Both tables change together or not at all, and you must use a single conditional UPDATE so that no overselling can happen.</li><li><code>update_seats(db, bus, new_seats, seen_version)</code>: admin edit with optimistic locking. If the bus is still at <code>seen_version</code>, set seats, increment version, and return True. If someone changed it first, return False.</li></ul>`,
  ex: [['book(db, 1, "sam")', 'True']], start: 'def book(db, bus, user):\n    pass\n\ndef update_seats(db, bus, new_seats, seen_version):\n    pass\n', nudge: [],
  sol: `def book(db, bus, user):\n    if db.execute("SELECT 1 FROM bookings WHERE bus_id = ? AND user = ?", (bus, user)).fetchone():\n        return False\n    with db:\n        if db.execute("UPDATE buses SET seats = seats - 1 WHERE id = ? AND seats > 0", (bus,)).rowcount != 1:\n            return False\n        db.execute("INSERT INTO bookings (bus_id, user) VALUES (?, ?)", (bus, user))\n    return True\n\ndef update_seats(db, bus, new_seats, seen_version):\n    with db:\n        cur = db.execute("UPDATE buses SET seats = ?, version = version + 1 WHERE id = ? AND version = ?", (new_seats, bus, seen_version))\n    return cur.rowcount == 1`, big: 'O(1) with indexes', idea: 'Put the check inside the UPDATE, so it can\'t be separated from the change.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.executescript("CREATE TABLE buses (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, seats INT, version INT DEFAULT 0);CREATE TABLE bookings (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, bus_id INT, user TEXT);INSERT INTO buses (id, seats) VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);")\nseats = lambda b: db.execute("SELECT seats FROM buses WHERE id = ?", (b,)).fetchone()[0]\ncount = lambda: db.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM bookings").fetchone()[0]\n'; return [['(book(db, 1, "sam"), seats(1), count())', '(True, 0, 1)', s], ['(book(db, 1, "sam"), book(db, 1, "ira"), seats(1), count())', '(True, False, 0, 1)', s], ['(book(db, 2, "sam"), book(db, 2, "sam"), seats(2))', '(True, False, 1)', s], ['(book(db, 9, "sam"), count())', '(False, 0)', s], ['(update_seats(db, 1, 5, 0), seats(1))', '(True, 5)', s], ['(update_seats(db, 1, 5, 0), update_seats(db, 1, 9, 0), seats(1))', '(True, False, 5)', s], ['update_seats(db, 1, 5, 7)', 'False', s],
    ['(book(db, 1, "sam"), seats(1))', '(False, 1)', s + 'db.execute("CREATE TRIGGER nope BEFORE INSERT ON bookings BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, \'x\'); END")\ndef _safe(f):\n    def g(*a):\n        try:\n            return f(*a)\n        except sqlite3.Error:\n            return False\n    return g\nbook = _safe(book)\n']]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'caching', phase: 5, title: 'Caching', mins: 9, read: [
`<p class="lede">A cache keeps a nearby copy of something slow to get. Your database might take 20 ms, your cache 0.2 ms. <b>That's a hundred times faster, in exchange for one hard problem: your copy might be wrong.</b></p>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [20, 50, 120, 60, 'request'], c: [240, 50, 130, 60, 'cache', 'y'], d: [520, 50, 150, 60, 'database\n(slow)', 'r'] }, [['a', 'c'], ['c', 'd', 'miss']]) + D.t(305, 130, 'hit: 0.2 ms', { a: 'middle', cls: 'g' }) + D.t(595, 130, 'then save a copy', { a: 'middle', cls: 'soft' }), 145),
`<h2>Keeping it small: LRU</h2><p>Memory is limited, so something has to go. <b>Least Recently Used</b> evicts whatever hasn't been touched for the longest.</p>`,
steps([
  { n: 'Room for 3. Most recent on the right.', d: D.cells(['a', 'b', 'c'], { x: 200, y: 30, w: 70 }) + D.t(40, 60, 'order', { cls: 'soft' }) },
  { n: 'Reading <code>a</code> makes it the most recent, so it moves to the right.', d: D.cells(['b', 'c', 'a'], { x: 200, y: 30, w: 70, hl: { 2: 'y' } }) + D.t(40, 60, 'get a', { cls: 'b' }) },
  { n: 'Adding <code>d</code> needs room, so the leftmost one goes: <code>b</code>.', d: D.cells(['c', 'a', 'd'], { x: 200, y: 30, w: 70, hl: { 2: 'g' } }) + D.t(40, 60, 'put d', { cls: 'b' }) + D.t(450, 60, 'b evicted', { cls: 'r' }) },
  { n: 'Everything must be O(1), which is the design puzzle: a hash map finds things instantly, and a linked list reorders instantly. Together they do both. Python\'s dicts remember insertion order, which helps.', d: D.flow({ a: [120, 20, 180, 60, 'hash map\nkey to node', 'y'], b: [400, 20, 200, 60, 'linked list\nrecency order', 'b'] }, [['a', 'b']]) }
], 110),
`<h2>Staleness: the hard part</h2>
<table><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How</th><th>Downside</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>TTL</b></td><td>Copies expire after N seconds</td><td>Wrong for up to N seconds</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Invalidate on write</b></td><td>When data changes, delete the cached copy</td><td>You have to find every place data changes</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Write through</b></td><td>Writes go to cache and database together</td><td>Slower writes</td></tr></table>
<h2>The stampede</h2>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [20, 10, 120, 36, 'req 1'], b: [20, 56, 120, 36, 'req 2'], c: [20, 102, 120, 36, 'req 1000', 'r'], d: [370, 40, 200, 70, 'database\nfalls over', 'r'] }, [['a', 'd'], ['b', 'd'], ['c', 'd']]) + D.t(600, 80, 'popular key expired', { cls: 'soft', size: 14 }), 150, 'A popular key expires, and a thousand requests all miss at the same moment. Fix: let one request recompute it while the others wait.'),
think('You cache a user\'s profile for 5 minutes. They change their name, and refresh. They see the old name. What is the simplest fix?', 'Delete that cache key when the name is written. TTL stays on as a safety net, in case some write path forgets.')
], core: [
{ id: 'lru-cache', title: 'LRU cache', diff: 'Medium', time: 18,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>LRU(capacity)</code>. <code>get(k)</code> returns the value or <code>None</code>, and counts as a use. <code>put(k, v)</code> adds or replaces, also counting as a use. When over capacity, evict the least recently used. Both must be O(1): tests do 500,000 operations with capacity 50,000.</p>`,
  ex: [['LRU(2): put a, put b, get a, put c, get b', 'None (b was evicted)']], start: 'class LRU:\n    ',
  nudge: ['What from the collections module remembers order and can move an item to the end in O(1)?'],
  sol: `from collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRU:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.cap = capacity\n        self.d = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, k):\n        if k not in self.d:\n            return None\n        self.d.move_to_end(k)\n        return self.d[k]\n\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        self.d[k] = v\n        self.d.move_to_end(k)\n        if len(self.d) > self.cap:\n            self.d.popitem(last=False)`, big: 'O(1) per operation', idea: 'A hash map plus an ordered list. Each does what the other can\'t.',
  tests: [['c.get("b")', 'None', 'c = LRU(2)\nc.put("a", 1)\nc.put("b", 2)\nc.get("a")\nc.put("c", 3)'], ['c.get("a")', '1', 'c = LRU(2)\nc.put("a", 1)\nc.put("b", 2)\nc.get("a")\nc.put("c", 3)'], ['c.get("a")', 'None', 'c = LRU(2)\nc.put("a", 1)\nc.put("b", 2)\nc.put("c", 3)'], ['c.get("b")', 'None', 'c = LRU(2)\nc.put("a", 1)\nc.put("b", 2)\nc.put("a", 9)\nc.put("c", 3)'], ['c.get("a")', '9', 'c = LRU(2)\nc.put("a", 1)\nc.put("a", 9)'], ['c.get("x")', 'None', 'c = LRU(1)'], ['(c.get(499999), c.get(0))', '(499999, None)', 'c = LRU(50000)\nfor i in range(500000):\n    c.put(i, i)']] },
{ id: 'ttl-cache', title: 'Cache with expiry', diff: 'Medium', time: 12,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>TTL(ttl, clock)</code>. <code>put(k, v)</code> stores. <code>get(k)</code> returns the value if less than <code>ttl</code> seconds old, otherwise <code>None</code>, and forgets it. <code>delete(k)</code> removes. <code>size()</code> counts stored items, and expired ones already noticed by a get must not count. Don't sleep: use <code>clock()</code>. A stored value of 0 or an empty string must still work.</p>`,
  ex: [['ttl = 10, put at t = 0, get at t = 10', 'None']], start: 'class TTL:\n    ',
  nudge: ['What do you need to store besides the value?', 'What does "if value:" do to a stored 0?'],
  sol: `class TTL:\n    def __init__(self, ttl, clock):\n        self.ttl, self.clock = ttl, clock\n        self.d = {}\n\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        self.d[k] = (v, self.clock() + self.ttl)\n\n    def get(self, k):\n        if k not in self.d:\n            return None\n        v, expires = self.d[k]\n        if self.clock() >= expires:\n            del self.d[k]\n            return None\n        return v\n\n    def delete(self, k):\n        self.d.pop(k, None)\n\n    def size(self):\n        return len(self.d)`, big: 'O(1)', idea: 'Store the expiry alongside the value. A clock passed in makes time testable.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'now = [0]\nc = TTL(10, lambda: now[0])\nc.put("a", 1)\n'; return [['c.get("a")', '1', s], ['c.get("a")', '1', s + 'now[0] = 9\n'], ['c.get("a")', 'None', s + 'now[0] = 10\n'], ['(c.get("a"), c.size())', '(None, 0)', s + 'now[0] = 10\n'], ['c.get("zz")', 'None', s], ['c.get("a")', 'None', s + 'c.delete("a")\n'], ['c.delete("zz")', 'None', s], ['c.get("z")', '0', s + 'c.put("z", 0)\n'], ['c.get("e")', '""', s + 'c.put("e", "")\n'], ['c.get("a")', '2', s + 'now[0] = 8\nc.put("a", 2)\nnow[0] = 15\n']]; })() }
], boss:
{ id: 'single-flight', title: 'Stop the stampede', diff: 'Hard', time: 22,
  desc: `<p>Requests arrive one at a time here (a simplified model of concurrency). Write <code>Loader(load, ttl, clock)</code> with <code>get(k)</code>.</p><ul><li>A fresh value is returned without calling <code>load</code>.</li><li>If the value is missing, call <code>load(k)</code> and cache it.</li><li><b>Stale while revalidate:</b> if the value is expired by less than <code>ttl</code> (so its age is in the range <code>[ttl, 2*ttl)</code>), return the old value <i>immediately</i> and put <code>k</code> in a refresh queue, once only however many times it's requested. <code>refresh()</code> reloads everything queued and returns how many it reloaded.</li><li>If it's older than that, treat it as missing.</li><li>If <code>load</code> raises during <code>get</code> with no usable value, the error reaches the caller and nothing is cached. If it raises during <code>refresh</code>, keep the old value, count that key as not reloaded, and carry on with the rest.</li></ul>`,
  ex: [['ttl 10: get at 0 loads; get at 12 returns old value and queues', 'refresh() returns 1']], start: 'class Loader:\n    ', nudge: [],
  sol: `class Loader:\n    def __init__(self, load, ttl, clock):\n        self.load, self.ttl, self.clock = load, ttl, clock\n        self.d = {}\n        self.queue = set()\n\n    def get(self, k):\n        now = self.clock()\n        if k in self.d:\n            v, at = self.d[k]\n            age = now - at\n            if age < self.ttl:\n                return v\n            if age < 2 * self.ttl:\n                self.queue.add(k)\n                return v\n        v = self.load(k)\n        self.d[k] = (v, now)\n        self.queue.discard(k)\n        return v\n\n    def refresh(self):\n        done = 0\n        for k in list(self.queue):\n            self.queue.discard(k)\n            try:\n                self.d[k] = (self.load(k), self.clock())\n                done += 1\n            except Exception:\n                pass\n        return done`, big: 'O(1) per get', idea: 'Serve a slightly old value at once, and refresh it once in the background. Users never wait, and the database never stampedes.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'now = [0]\ncalls = []\ndef load(k):\n    calls.append(k)\n    if k == "bad" or fail[0]:\n        raise RuntimeError("db down")\n    return k + str(len(calls))\nfail = [False]\nL = Loader(load, 10, lambda: now[0])\n'; return [['(L.get("a"), L.get("a"), len(calls))', '("a1", "a1", 1)', s], ['(L.get("a"), len(calls))', '("a1", 1)', s + 'L.get("a")\nnow[0] = 12\n'], ['(L.get("a"), L.get("a"), L.refresh(), L.get("a"), len(calls))', '("a1", "a1", 1, "a2", 2)', s + 'L.get("a")\nnow[0] = 12\n'], ['L.refresh()', '0', s], ['(L.get("a"), len(calls))', '("a2", 2)', s + 'L.get("a")\nnow[0] = 20\n'], ['raised and "bad" not in L.d', 'True', s + 'try:\n    L.get("bad")\n    raised = False\nexcept RuntimeError:\n    raised = True\n'], ['(L.refresh(), L.get("a"))', '(0, "a1")', s + 'L.get("a")\nnow[0] = 12\nL.get("a")\nfail[0] = True\n'], ['L.refresh()', '1', s + 'L.get("a")\nL.get("b")\nnow[0] = 12\nL.get("a")\nL.get("a")\n']]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'rate-limits', phase: 5, title: 'Rate limiting and retries', mins: 8, read: [
`<p class="lede">Systems don't usually die from bad code. They die from <b>too much traffic, or from clients retrying in a storm.</b> Rate limits protect you from others, and backoff protects others from you.</p>`,
`<h2>The token bucket</h2>`,
steps([
  { n: 'A bucket holds up to 4 tokens. Each request takes one. Tokens drip back in at a steady rate.', d: D.cells(['●', '●', '●', '●'], { x: 200, y: 30, hl: { 0: 'g', 1: 'g', 2: 'g', 3: 'g' } }) + D.t(40, 60, 'full', {}) },
  { n: 'A burst of 3 requests: all fine, and the bucket drains. Bursts are allowed, up to the bucket\'s size.', d: D.cells(['●', '', '', ''], { x: 200, y: 30, hl: { 0: 'g' } }) + D.t(40, 60, 'after burst', {}) },
  { n: 'Two more right away. The second one finds it empty and gets 429 Too Many Requests.', d: D.cells(['', '', '', ''], { x: 200, y: 30 }) + D.t(40, 60, 'empty', { cls: 'r' }) + D.t(440, 60, '429', { cls: 'r', size: 24 }) },
  { n: 'A trick: no timer is needed. When a request arrives, work out how many tokens would have dripped in since last time. That\'s just (time passed) × rate.', d: D.cells(['●', '●', '', ''], { x: 200, y: 30, hl: { 0: 'y', 1: 'y' } }) + D.t(40, 60, '2 s × 1/s', {}) }
], 110),
`<h2>Retries can make everything worse</h2>`,
fig(D.plot({ x: 50, y: 12, w: 280, h: 130, xr: [0, 6], yr: [0, 10], xlab: 'time', ylab: 'load', pts: [[1, 9, 'r'], [2, 9, 'r'], [3, 9, 'r'], [4, 9, 'r'], [5, 9, 'r']] }) + D.plot({ x: 410, y: 12, w: 280, h: 130, xr: [0, 6], yr: [0, 10], xlab: 'time', ylab: 'load', pts: [[0.7, 2, 'g'], [1.4, 3, 'g'], [2.2, 1, 'g'], [3.1, 2, 'g'], [4.5, 1, 'g'], [5.4, 2, 'g']] }), 175, 'Left: everyone retries at once, on the same schedule, and the server never recovers. Right: backoff with jitter spreads them out.'),
`<h2>Backoff with jitter</h2><ul><li><b>Exponential:</b> wait 1 s, then 2, 4, 8. Give the system room to recover.</li><li><b>Cap it,</b> and limit the number of attempts.</li><li><b>Jitter:</b> add randomness, or all your clients retry in sync, like a stampede.</li><li><b>Only retry what could work:</b> a 503 or timeout, yes. A 400? It'll fail the same way forever.</li></ul>
<h2>Idempotency</h2><p>You sent "charge $50", then the connection dropped. Did it work? Retrying might charge twice. The fix: the client sends a unique key with the request. The server stores the result per key, and repeats return the stored result instead of redoing the work.</p>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [20, 40, 170, 60, 'POST /pay\nkey: abc123', 'y'], b: [270, 40, 170, 60, 'seen abc123\nbefore?'], c: [520, 8, 180, 50, 'yes: same old result', 'g'], d: [520, 80, 180, 50, 'no: do it, store it', 'b'] }, [['a', 'b'], ['b', 'c'], ['b', 'd']]), 145),
think('Which HTTP methods are already meant to be idempotent, and which needs extra help?', 'GET, PUT and DELETE are meant to be safe to repeat. POST is not, which is why payment APIs ask for an idempotency key.')
], core: [
{ id: 'token-bucket', title: 'Token bucket', diff: 'Medium', time: 14,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>Bucket(capacity, rate, clock)</code>. Starts full. <code>allow()</code> returns True and uses a token if at least one's available, otherwise False. Tokens refill continuously at <code>rate</code> per second, never above capacity. Don't use timers or sleep.</p>`,
  ex: [['Bucket(2, 1, clock): allow, allow, allow at t = 0', 'True, True, False']], start: 'class Bucket:\n    ',
  nudge: ['What two numbers do you need to remember between calls?'],
  sol: `class Bucket:\n    def __init__(self, capacity, rate, clock):\n        self.cap, self.rate, self.clock = capacity, rate, clock\n        self.tokens = capacity\n        self.last = clock()\n\n    def allow(self):\n        now = self.clock()\n        self.tokens = min(self.cap, self.tokens + (now - self.last) * self.rate)\n        self.last = now\n        if self.tokens >= 1:\n            self.tokens -= 1\n            return True\n        return False`, big: 'O(1) time and space per client', idea: 'Compute what would have dripped in, at the moment you need it.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'now = [0.0]\nb = Bucket(2, 1, lambda: now[0])\n'; return [['[b.allow(), b.allow(), b.allow()]', '[True, True, False]', s], ['b.allow()', 'True', s + 'b.allow()\nb.allow()\nnow[0] = 1.0\n'], ['b.allow()', 'False', s + 'b.allow()\nb.allow()\nnow[0] = 0.5\n'], ['[b.allow(), b.allow(), b.allow()]', '[True, True, False]', s + 'now[0] = 100.0\n'], ['[b.allow(), b.allow()]', '[True, False]', s + 'b.allow()\nb.allow()\nnow[0] = 0.5\nb.allow()\nnow[0] = 1.0\n']]; })() },
{ id: 'backoff', title: 'Retry politely', diff: 'Medium', time: 14,
  desc: `<p><code>retry(call, sleep, attempts=5, base=1, cap=16)</code>. Run <code>call()</code>. On success, return its result. On <code>TemporaryError</code> (a predefined exception in the tests), wait, then try again, up to <code>attempts</code> total calls. Any other exception is raised right away, with no retry. If all attempts fail, re-raise the last error. The wait before retry <code>i</code> (from 0) is a random amount from 0 up to <code>min(cap, base * 2**i)</code>, full jitter. Call <code>sleep(seconds)</code>, and don't sleep after the final failure.</p>`,
  ex: [['fails twice, then succeeds', 'returns the result, with 2 sleeps']], start: 'import random\n\ndef retry(call, sleep, attempts=5, base=1, cap=16):\n    ',
  nudge: ['How do you avoid a sleep after the last failure?'],
  sol: `import random\n\ndef retry(call, sleep, attempts=5, base=1, cap=16):\n    for i in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return call()\n        except TemporaryError:\n            if i == attempts - 1:\n                raise\n            sleep(random.uniform(0, min(cap, base * 2 ** i)))`, big: 'O(attempts)', idea: 'Back off exponentially, randomise, cap, and only retry what could succeed.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'class TemporaryError(Exception):\n    pass\nslept = []\ncalls = [0]\ndef flaky(n):\n    def f():\n        calls[0] += 1\n        if calls[0] <= n:\n            raise TemporaryError("try later")\n        return "ok"\n    return f\n'; return [['retry(flaky(0), slept.append)', '"ok"', s], ['(retry(flaky(2), slept.append), len(slept))', '("ok", 2)', s], ['raised and calls[0] == 3 and len(slept) == 2', 'True', s + 'try:\n    retry(flaky(9), slept.append, attempts=3)\n    raised = False\nexcept TemporaryError:\n    raised = True\n'], ['raised and calls[0] == 1 and not slept', 'True', s + 'def nope():\n    calls[0] += 1\n    raise ValueError("bad")\ntry:\n    retry(nope, slept.append)\n    raised = False\nexcept ValueError:\n    raised = True\n'], ['all(0 <= t <= lim for t, lim in zip(slept, [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 16, 16]))', 'True', s + 'retry(flaky(7), slept.append, attempts=8)\n'], ['len(set(slept)) > 5', 'True', s + 'retry(flaky(7), slept.append, attempts=8)\n'], ['max(slept) > 4', 'True', s + 'for _ in range(50):\n    calls[0] = 0\n    retry(flaky(7), slept.append, attempts=8)\n']]; })() }
], boss:
{ id: 'idempotent-pay', title: 'Never charge twice', diff: 'Hard', time: 22,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>Payments(charge)</code>, where <code>charge(amount)</code> calls an outside provider, returning a receipt string or raising an exception. <code>pay(key, amount)</code>:</p><ul><li>First time for a key: calls charge, stores and returns <code>(200, receipt)</code>.</li><li>Same key and amount again: returns the same result without charging.</li><li>Same key with a <b>different</b> amount: returns <code>(422, "key reused")</code>, without charging.</li><li>If charge raises, returns <code>(502, "provider failed")</code>, and does <b>not</b> remember the key, so a retry can try again. A retry with a different amount is allowed after a failure.</li><li>If <code>charge</code> calls <code>pay</code> again with the same key while running (a duplicate arriving mid-flight), that inner call returns <code>(409, "in progress")</code> without charging.</li></ul>`,
  ex: [['pay("k", 5) twice', 'one charge, same receipt twice']], start: 'class Payments:\n    ', nudge: [],
  sol: `class Payments:\n    def __init__(self, charge):\n        self.charge = charge\n        self.seen = {}\n\n    def pay(self, key, amount):\n        if key in self.seen:\n            state, amt, result = self.seen[key]\n            if amt != amount:\n                return (422, "key reused")\n            if state == "running":\n                return (409, "in progress")\n            return result\n        self.seen[key] = ("running", amount, None)\n        try:\n            receipt = self.charge(amount)\n        except Exception:\n            del self.seen[key]\n            return (502, "provider failed")\n        result = (200, receipt)\n        self.seen[key] = ("done", amount, result)\n        return result`, big: 'O(1)', idea: 'Three states per key: running, done, or never seen. Record the key before the outside call.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'n = [0]\nfail = [False]\ndef charge(a):\n    if fail[0]:\n        raise RuntimeError("down")\n    n[0] += 1\n    return "r" + str(n[0])\nP = Payments(charge)\n'; return [['P.pay("k", 5)', '(200, "r1")', s], ['(P.pay("k", 5), P.pay("k", 5), n[0])', '((200, "r1"), (200, "r1"), 1)', s], ['(P.pay("a", 5), P.pay("b", 5), n[0])', '((200, "r1"), (200, "r2"), 2)', s], ['(P.pay("k", 5), P.pay("k", 9), n[0])', '((200, "r1"), (422, "key reused"), 1)', s], ['P.pay("k", 5)', '(502, "provider failed")', s + 'fail[0] = True\n'], ['P.pay("k", 5)', '(200, "r1")', s + 'fail[0] = True\nP.pay("k", 5)\nfail[0] = False\n'], ['P.pay("k", 9)', '(200, "r1")', s + 'fail[0] = True\nP.pay("k", 5)\nfail[0] = False\n'], ['(inner, outer, count[0])', '([(409, "in progress")], (200, "x"), 1)', 'count = [0]\ninner = []\ndef c(a):\n    count[0] += 1\n    inner.append(P.pay("k", a))\n    return "x"\nP = Payments(c)\nouter = P.pay("k", 5)\n']]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'queues-workers', phase: 5, title: 'Background jobs', mins: 8, read: [
`<p class="lede">Sending an email takes 2 seconds. A user shouldn't wait for that. <b>Write down the job, answer immediately, and let a worker do it later.</b> Every serious backend has this shape.</p>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [10, 50, 130, 60, 'web request'], q: [230, 40, 210, 80, 'queue of jobs', 'y'], w1: [540, 10, 160, 50, 'worker'], w2: [540, 100, 160, 50, 'worker'] }, [['a', 'q', 'enqueue'], ['q', 'w1'], ['q', 'w2']]) + D.t(75, 135, 'answers in 5 ms', { a: 'middle', cls: 'g' }), 160, 'Traffic spikes just make the queue longer. Nothing falls over, and you can add workers.'),
`<h2>What can go wrong</h2><p>Picture a worker that takes a job, does the work, and then crashes before marking it done. What should happen?</p>`,
steps([
  { n: 'A worker claims a job. It stays in the queue, marked as in progress, with a time limit. It isn\'t deleted yet.', d: D.flow({ j: [90, 40, 200, 60, 'job: in progress', 'y'], w: [420, 40, 180, 60, 'worker'] }, [['w', 'j', 'claims']]) },
  { n: 'Normal case: the worker finishes and acknowledges it. Only now is it removed.', d: D.flow({ j: [90, 40, 200, 60, 'job: done', 'g'], w: [420, 40, 180, 60, 'worker'] }, [['w', 'j', 'ack']]) },
  { n: 'Crash case: no acknowledgement arrives, and the time limit passes. The job goes back in the queue for someone else. No job is ever lost.', d: D.flow({ j: [90, 40, 200, 60, 'job: back in queue', 'b'], w: [420, 40, 180, 60, 'worker crashed', 'r'] }) },
  { n: 'But the worker might have finished the work, and died just before the ack. So the job will run twice. That is "at least once" delivery. So <b>jobs must be idempotent</b>, safe to run twice. You know how to do that.', d: D.t(120, 60, 'at least once, not exactly once', { cls: 'r', size: 24 }) + D.t(120, 100, 'design every job to be safe to repeat', { cls: 'g', size: 20 }) }
], 130),
`<h2>Poison jobs</h2><p>A job that always crashes will loop forever, blocking everything. So count attempts. After N failures, move it to a <b>dead-letter queue</b> for a human to look at.</p>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [40, 40, 160, 60, 'job fails'], b: [280, 40, 160, 60, 'tries < max?', 'y'], c: [530, 4, 170, 50, 'retry with backoff', 'b'], d: [530, 80, 170, 50, 'dead letters', 'r'] }, [['a', 'b'], ['b', 'c', 'yes'], ['b', 'd', 'no']]), 140),
think('Should a job be enqueued before or after the database commit? What can go wrong with each?', 'Before: the worker may run before the data exists, or the commit fails and the job is bogus. After: a crash in between loses the job. The sturdy fix is an outbox: write the job to a table in the same transaction, and have another process send it.')
], core: [
{ id: 'job-queue', title: 'A reliable queue', diff: 'Hard', time: 25,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>Queue(clock, visibility=30, max_tries=3)</code>.</p><ul><li><code>push(job)</code> adds a job and returns its id (from 1). </li><li><code>claim()</code> returns <code>(id, job)</code> for the oldest available job, or <code>None</code>. It's then invisible for <code>visibility</code> seconds. A job whose claim has timed out becomes available again.</li><li><code>ack(id)</code> marks it finished. Returns False if it isn't currently claimed.</li><li><code>fail(id)</code> makes it available again immediately.</li><li>A job that has been claimed <code>max_tries</code> times and then fails or times out goes to <code>dead()</code>, a list of jobs, instead of being available again.</li></ul>`,
  ex: [['push("a"); claim()', '(1, "a")']], start: 'class Queue:\n    ',
  nudge: ['What does a job record need to hold? List the fields first.', 'Timeouts: do you need a timer, or can you check when claim is called?'],
  sol: `class Queue:\n    def __init__(self, clock, visibility=30, max_tries=3):\n        self.clock, self.vis, self.max = clock, visibility, max_tries\n        self.jobs = {}\n        self.next_id = 1\n        self.dead_jobs = []\n\n    def push(self, job):\n        id = self.next_id\n        self.next_id += 1\n        self.jobs[id] = {"job": job, "until": None, "tries": 0}\n        return id\n\n    def _reap(self):\n        now = self.clock()\n        for id, j in list(self.jobs.items()):\n            if j["until"] is not None and now >= j["until"]:\n                self._release(id)\n\n    def _release(self, id):\n        j = self.jobs[id]\n        j["until"] = None\n        if j["tries"] >= self.max:\n            self.dead_jobs.append(j["job"])\n            del self.jobs[id]\n\n    def claim(self):\n        self._reap()\n        for id, j in self.jobs.items():\n            if j["until"] is None:\n                j["until"] = self.clock() + self.vis\n                j["tries"] += 1\n                return (id, j["job"])\n        return None\n\n    def ack(self, id):\n        self._reap()\n        if id in self.jobs and self.jobs[id]["until"] is not None:\n            del self.jobs[id]\n            return True\n        return False\n\n    def fail(self, id):\n        if id in self.jobs and self.jobs[id]["until"] is not None:\n            self._release(id)\n\n    def dead(self):\n        self._reap()\n        return list(self.dead_jobs)`, big: 'O(n) per claim here. A real system indexes by availability.', idea: 'Never delete on claim. A job leaves only by ack or the dead-letter queue.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'now = [0]\nq = Queue(lambda: now[0], visibility=30, max_tries=3)\n'; return [['(q.push("a"), q.push("b"))', '(1, 2)', s], ['q.claim()', 'None', s], ['(q.claim(), q.claim(), q.claim())', '((1, "a"), (2, "b"), None)', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.push("b")\n'], ['q.claim()', '(1, "a")', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.claim()\nnow[0] = 30\n'], ['q.claim()', 'None', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.claim()\nnow[0] = 29\n'], ['(q.ack(1), q.claim())', '(True, None)', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.claim()\n'], ['q.ack(1)', 'False', s + 'q.push("a")\n'], ['q.ack(1)', 'False', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.claim()\nq.ack(1)\n'], ['q.ack(1)', 'False', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.claim()\nnow[0] = 40\n'], ['q.claim()', '(1, "a")', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.claim()\nq.fail(1)\n'], ['(q.claim(), q.dead())', '(None, ["a"])', s + 'q.push("a")\nfor _ in range(3):\n    q.claim()\n    q.fail(1)\n'], ['(q.claim(), q.dead())', '(None, ["a"])', s + 'q.push("a")\nfor i in range(3):\n    q.claim()\n    now[0] += 30\n'], ['q.claim()', '(1, "a")', s + 'q.push("a")\nq.claim()\nq.fail(1)\nq.claim()\nq.fail(1)\n']]; })() }
], boss:
{ id: 'worker-loop', title: 'Worker with an outbox', diff: 'Hard', time: 25,
  desc: `<p>In SQLite: table <code>orders(id, item)</code> and <code>outbox(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, order_id, sent INT DEFAULT 0)</code>.</p><ul><li><code>place_order(db, item)</code> inserts the order <b>and</b> an outbox row in one transaction, and returns the order id. If the second insert fails, neither may remain, and the error reaches the caller.</li><li><code>drain(db, send)</code> goes through unsent outbox rows in id order, calling <code>send(order_id)</code>. Mark a row sent only after <code>send</code> returns. If <code>send</code> raises, stop at that row, leaving it and later ones unsent, and return how many were sent. Marks already made must stay, even when a later one fails.</li></ul>`,
  ex: [['place_order(db, "pen"); drain(db, send)', '1']], start: 'def place_order(db, item):\n    pass\n\ndef drain(db, send):\n    pass\n', nudge: [],
  sol: `def place_order(db, item):\n    with db:\n        cur = db.execute("INSERT INTO orders (item) VALUES (?)", (item,))\n        order_id = cur.lastrowid\n        db.execute("INSERT INTO outbox (order_id) VALUES (?)", (order_id,))\n    return order_id\n\ndef drain(db, send):\n    rows = db.execute("SELECT id, order_id FROM outbox WHERE sent = 0 ORDER BY id").fetchall()\n    done = 0\n    for id, order_id in rows:\n        try:\n            send(order_id)\n        except Exception:\n            break\n        with db:\n            db.execute("UPDATE outbox SET sent = 1 WHERE id = ?", (id,))\n        done += 1\n    return done`, big: 'O(pending rows)', idea: 'Commit the fact and the intent together. Delivery happens after, and can repeat safely.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.executescript("CREATE TABLE orders (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, item TEXT);CREATE TABLE outbox (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, order_id INT, sent INT DEFAULT 0);")\nsent = []\nn = lambda t: db.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM " + t).fetchone()[0]\nunsent = lambda: db.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM outbox WHERE sent = 0").fetchone()[0]\n'; const bad = 'def flaky(o):\n    if o == 2:\n        raise RuntimeError("down")\n    sent.append(o)\n'; return [['(place_order(db, "pen"), n("orders"), n("outbox"))', '(1, 1, 1)', s], ['(drain(db, sent.append), sent, unsent())', '(2, [1, 2], 0)', s + 'place_order(db, "a")\nplace_order(db, "b")\n'], ['(drain(db, sent.append), drain(db, sent.append), sent)', '(1, 0, [1])', s + 'place_order(db, "a")\n'], ['(drain(db, flaky), sent, unsent())', '(1, [1], 2)', s + bad + 'for x in "abc":\n    place_order(db, x)\n'], ['(n("orders"), n("outbox"), raised)', '(0, 0, True)', s + 'db.execute("CREATE TRIGGER x BEFORE INSERT ON outbox BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, \'no\'); END")\ntry:\n    place_order(db, "a")\n    raised = False\nexcept sqlite3.Error:\n    raised = True\n'], ['(db.rollback(), unsent())[1]', '2', s + bad + 'for x in "abc":\n    place_order(db, x)\ndrain(db, flaky)\n']]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'observability', phase: 5, title: 'Seeing what your system does', mins: 7, read: [
`<p class="lede">When production breaks at 3 am, you can't add a <code>print</code> and re-run. <b>You can only look at what you recorded beforehand.</b></p>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [10, 20, 220, 110, 'Logs\nwhat happened,\nfor one request', 'y'], b: [250, 20, 220, 110, 'Metrics\nhow much, how fast,\nover time', 'b'], c: [490, 20, 220, 110, 'Traces\nwhere the time went,\nacross services', 'g'] }), 145),
`<h2>Averages lie</h2><p>The average latency is 100 ms. Everybody's fine? Look at this:</p>`,
fig(D.bars([1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 9], { x: 60, y: 150, w: 40, unit: 14, labels: false, hl: { 9: 'r' } }) + D.t(480, 60, 'average looks fine', {}) + D.t(480, 90, 'but 1 in 10 users wait 9×', { cls: 'r' }) + D.t(480, 130, 'p90, p99: what the slowest', {}) + D.t(480, 152, 'users actually feel', {}), 170),
`<p>Percentiles fix this: p99 is the value 99% of requests beat. Users make lots of requests, so nearly everyone regularly hits your p99. That's what to watch.</p>
<h2>What to measure</h2><p>For any service, start with four: <b>traffic</b>, <b>errors</b>, <b>latency</b>, and <b>saturation</b> (how full you are).</p>
<h2>Logs machines can read</h2>`,
`<pre><code># hard to search
print("user 7 failed to pay 50")

# easy to search, count and alert on
log({"event": "payment_failed", "user_id": 7, "amount": 50, "request_id": "r-91f"})</code></pre>`,
`<p>A <b>request id</b> stamped on every log line lets you follow one request all the way through. And <b>never log secrets</b>: passwords and tokens in logs have caused plenty of real breaches.</p>`,
think('Your dashboard shows 0 errors, but users are complaining. What could cause that?', 'The errors may be happening before your code, like at a load balancer, or be returned as 200 with an error inside. Also, a slow response is a failure to a user, without being an error at all.')
], core: [
{ id: 'percentile', title: 'p95 latency', diff: 'Easy', time: 8,
  desc: `<p>Return the value at percentile <code>p</code> using the nearest-rank method: sort the values, then take the item at rank <code>ceil(p / 100 × n)</code>, counting ranks from 1. An empty list returns <code>None</code>. The input list must not change.</p>`,
  ex: [['[15, 20, 35, 40, 50], 40', '20'], ['[1], 99', '1']], start: 'import math\n\ndef percentile(values, p):\n    ',
  nudge: ['Ranks start at 1 and Python positions start at 0. What is the conversion?'],
  sol: `import math\n\ndef percentile(values, p):\n    if not values:\n        return None\n    s = sorted(values)\n    return s[max(0, math.ceil(p / 100 * len(s)) - 1)]`, big: 'O(n log n)', idea: 'Sort and take a rank. Watch the off-by-one.',
  tests: [['percentile([15, 20, 35, 40, 50], 40)', '20'], ['percentile([1], 99)', '1'], ['percentile([], 50)', 'None'], ['percentile(list(range(1, 101)), 95)', '95'], ['percentile([5, 1, 3], 100)', '5'], ['percentile([5, 1, 3], 0)', '1'], ['(percentile(a, 50), a)', '(2, [3, 1, 2])', 'a = [3, 1, 2]']] },
{ id: 'redact-logs', title: 'Keep secrets out of logs', diff: 'Easy', time: 10,
  desc: `<p><code>redact(event)</code> returns a <b>copy</b> of a nested dict, or list of dicts, with the values of sensitive keys replaced by <code>"***"</code>. A key is sensitive if its lowercase name contains <code>password</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>secret</code> or <code>authorization</code>. Other values are unchanged. It must work at any depth. The original isn't changed.</p>`,
  ex: [['{"user": 7, "auth": {"Token": "abc"}}', '{"user": 7, "auth": {"Token": "***"}}']], start: 'def redact(event):\n    ',
  nudge: ['What shape is the data? What does that suggest for the code?'],
  sol: `SENSITIVE = ("password", "token", "secret", "authorization")\n\ndef redact(event):\n    if isinstance(event, dict):\n        return {k: "***" if any(s in str(k).lower() for s in SENSITIVE) else redact(v) for k, v in event.items()}\n    if isinstance(event, list):\n        return [redact(x) for x in event]\n    return event`, big: 'O(size)', idea: 'Nested shape, recursive code. Treat secrets at one choke point.',
  tests: [['redact({"user": 7, "auth": {"Token": "abc"}})', '{"user": 7, "auth": {"Token": "***"}}'], ['redact({"a": [{"password": 1}, {"b": 2}]})', '{"a": [{"password": "***"}, {"b": 2}]}'], ['redact(5)', '5'], ['redact({"my_API_Secret_key": "x"})', '{"my_API_Secret_key": "***"}'], ['(redact(e), e)', '({"token": "***"}, {"token": "t"})', 'e = {"token": "t"}'], ['redact([{"Authorization": "Bearer x"}])', '[{"Authorization": "***"}]'], ['redact({"password": {"deep": 1}})', '{"password": "***"}']] }
], boss:
{ id: 'metrics-window', title: 'Live error rate alarm', diff: 'Hard', time: 22,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>Monitor(window, clock)</code> tracks results over the last <code>window</code> seconds. <code>record(ok, ms)</code> adds a result. <code>error_rate()</code> returns failures ÷ total in the window, or 0.0 if none. <code>p99()</code> returns nearest-rank p99 latency in the window, or <code>None</code>. <code>alarm(min_count, threshold)</code> returns True if the window has at least <code>min_count</code> results and the error rate is at or above <code>threshold</code>. A result counts while <code>now - t &lt; window</code>. Tests record 200,000 results with error_rate polled often, so <code>error_rate</code> mustn't scan the whole window each time.</p>`,
  ex: [['two oks and one fail', 'error_rate() is about 0.333']], start: 'class Monitor:\n    ', nudge: [],
  sol: `import math\nfrom collections import deque\n\nclass Monitor:\n    def __init__(self, window, clock):\n        self.window, self.clock = window, clock\n        self.q = deque()\n        self.fails = 0\n\n    def _trim(self):\n        now = self.clock()\n        while self.q and now - self.q[0][0] >= self.window:\n            _, ok, _ms = self.q.popleft()\n            if not ok:\n                self.fails -= 1\n\n    def record(self, ok, ms):\n        self.q.append((self.clock(), ok, ms))\n        if not ok:\n            self.fails += 1\n\n    def error_rate(self):\n        self._trim()\n        return self.fails / len(self.q) if self.q else 0.0\n\n    def p99(self):\n        self._trim()\n        if not self.q:\n            return None\n        s = sorted(m for _, _, m in self.q)\n        return s[math.ceil(0.99 * len(s)) - 1]\n\n    def alarm(self, min_count, threshold):\n        rate = self.error_rate()\n        return len(self.q) >= min_count and rate >= threshold`, big: 'O(1) amortised for error_rate; p99 sorts the window', idea: 'A sliding window over time. A running count avoids rescanning.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'now = [0]\nm = Monitor(60, lambda: now[0])\n'; return [['m.error_rate()', '0.0', s], ['m.p99()', 'None', s], ['m.error_rate()', 'approx(1 / 3)', s + 'm.record(True, 1)\nm.record(True, 1)\nm.record(False, 1)\n'], ['m.error_rate()', '0.0', s + 'm.record(False, 1)\nnow[0] = 60\n'], ['m.error_rate()', '1.0', s + 'm.record(False, 1)\nnow[0] = 59\n'], ['m.p99()', '198', s + 'for i in range(1, 201):\n    m.record(True, i)\n'], ['m.p99()', '99', s + 'for i in range(1, 100):\n    m.record(True, i)\n'], ['m.alarm(3, 0.5)', 'False', s + 'm.record(False, 1)\nm.record(False, 1)\n'], ['m.alarm(2, 0.5)', 'True', s + 'm.record(False, 1)\nm.record(True, 1)\n'], ['m.alarm(1, 0.5)', 'False', s + 'm.record(False, 1)\nnow[0] = 99\n'], ['sum(1 for i in range(200000) if m.record(i % 2 == 0, 1) is None and m.error_rate() >= 0)', '200000', s]]; })() }
});

unit({ id: 'system-design', phase: 5, title: 'System design', mins: 12, read: [
`<p class="lede">System design means deciding <b>which things fail, and how you'll live with that</b>. There's no right answer, only trade-offs you can defend. Your rough pad is your main tool here.</p>
<h2>A way to think through any system</h2>`,
fig(D.flow({ a: [6, 20, 130, 66, '1. Pin it down\nwhat, how much', 'y'], b: [150, 20, 130, 66, '2. Estimate\nnumbers'], c: [294, 20, 130, 66, '3. Draw boxes\nhappy path'], d: [438, 20, 130, 66, '4. Find the\nbottleneck', 'r'], e: [582, 20, 130, 66, '5. What breaks?\nfailure modes'] }, [['a', 'b'], ['b', 'c'], ['c', 'd'], ['d', 'e']]), 105),
`<h2>Numbers worth knowing</h2><table><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Rough time</th></tr><tr><td>Memory read</td><td>100 ns</td></tr><tr><td>SSD read</td><td>100 µs (1,000 × slower)</td></tr><tr><td>Same-datacenter round trip</td><td>0.5 ms</td></tr><tr><td>Across a continent</td><td>50-100 ms</td></tr></table><p>A day is about 100,000 seconds. So a million requests a day is only about 10 per second on average, with peaks perhaps ten times that. Plain Postgres and one server handle far more than people think.</p>
<h2>Scaling reads, then writes</h2>`,
steps([
  { n: 'Start: one server, one database. Simple, cheap, and enough for most products for a long while. Don\'t skip this step.', d: D.flow({ a: [120, 40, 180, 60, 'app'], b: [420, 40, 180, 60, 'database'] }, [['a', 'b']]) },
  { n: 'More traffic: make app servers stateless, then run several behind a load balancer. Any server can answer any request. That\'s why session data has to live elsewhere.', d: D.flow({ l: [10, 60, 130, 60, 'load\nbalancer', 'y'], a: [220, 10, 150, 50, 'app'], b: [220, 70, 150, 50, 'app'], c: [220, 130, 150, 50, 'app'], d: [480, 70, 180, 50, 'database'] }, [['l', 'a'], ['l', 'b'], ['l', 'c'], ['b', 'd']]) },
  { n: 'Too many reads: add a cache and read replicas. Replicas lag slightly behind, so a user might not see their own write right away, unless you read that from the primary.', d: D.flow({ a: [20, 70, 130, 50, 'apps'], c: [240, 10, 160, 50, 'cache', 'y'], p: [240, 70, 160, 50, 'primary'], r: [240, 130, 160, 50, 'replicas', 'b'] }, [['a', 'c'], ['a', 'p'], ['a', 'r']]) + D.t(430, 100, 'all writes go here', { cls: 'soft' }) },
  { n: 'Too many writes, or too much data: shard it, splitting rows across databases by a key. It\'s powerful, and also painful: joins across shards get hard. Treat it as a last resort.', d: D.flow({ a: [20, 70, 130, 50, 'router', 'y'], x: [260, 10, 200, 50, 'users A-H'], y: [260, 70, 200, 50, 'users I-P'], z: [260, 130, 200, 50, 'users Q-Z'] }, [['a', 'x'], ['a', 'y'], ['a', 'z']]) }
], 190),
`<h2>The trade-off you can't avoid</h2><p>When the network splits, one side can't talk to the other. Do you refuse requests (<b>consistency</b>) or answer with possibly old data (<b>availability</b>)? A bank chooses the first. A like counter chooses the second.</p>
<h2>Consistent hashing</h2><p>A plain <code>hash(key) % n</code> breaks when n changes: nearly every key moves. Picture servers on a ring instead. A key belongs to the next server clockwise. Adding a server only takes keys from one neighbour.</p>`,
fig(D.circle(190, 95, 76) + D.graph({ A: [190, 19], B: [266, 95], C: [190, 171] }, [], { r: 15, hl: { A: 'y', B: 'y', C: 'y' } }) + `<circle class="dot r" cx="243" cy="41" r="5"/><circle class="dot r" cx="125" cy="140" r="5"/>` + D.t(250, 30, 'key: goes to B', { cls: 'r', size: 14 }) + D.t(390, 70, 'Add a server: only the keys in', {}) + D.t(390, 96, 'one slice of the ring move.', {}) + D.t(390, 136, 'With %, nearly all of them would.', { cls: 'r' }), 190),
think('Design a URL shortener on the pad. What is the read-to-write ratio like? Where would you put a cache? How would you generate short codes?', 'Reads vastly outnumber writes, so cache lookups aggressively. For codes, a counter in base 62 gives short, unique ones. But counters are guessable, so scramble them if that matters. Random codes need a collision check.')
], core: [
{ id: 'base62', title: 'Short codes', diff: 'Easy', time: 10,
  desc: `<p>For a URL shortener: <code>encode(n)</code> turns a non-negative integer into a base 62 string using <code>0-9a-zA-Z</code>, in that order. <code>decode(s)</code> reverses it. <code>encode(0)</code> is <code>"0"</code>.</p>`,
  ex: [['61', '"Z"'], ['62', '"10"']], start: 'def encode(n):\n    pass\n\ndef decode(s):\n    pass\n',
  nudge: ['How do you get the digits of a number in base 10? What changes for base 62?'],
  sol: `ALPHA = "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ"\n\ndef encode(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    out = []\n    while n:\n        n, r = divmod(n, 62)\n        out.append(ALPHA[r])\n    return "".join(reversed(out))\n\ndef decode(s):\n    v = 0\n    for c in s:\n        v = v * 62 + ALPHA.index(c)\n    return v`, big: 'O(log n)', idea: 'Same as writing a number in any base.',
  tests: [['encode(61)', '"Z"'], ['encode(62)', '"10"'], ['encode(0)', '"0"'], ['decode("10")', '62'], ['all(decode(encode(i)) == i for i in range(5000))', 'True'], ['decode(encode(10**15))', '10**15'], ['encode(35)', '"z"'], ['len(encode(62**7 - 1))', '7']] }
], boss:
{ id: 'consistent-hash', title: 'Consistent hashing', diff: 'Hard', time: 25,
  desc: `<p>Class <code>Ring(replicas=100)</code>. Each server is placed at <code>replicas</code> points on the ring, to even out the load. <code>add(server)</code>, <code>remove(server)</code>, and <code>get(key)</code>, which returns the server at the first point at or after the key's hash, wrapping around, or <code>None</code> if the ring's empty. Hash with <code>int(hashlib.md5(text.encode()).hexdigest(), 16)</code>, since Python's <code>hash</code> differs between runs. Use <code>bisect</code>. Tests check that adding a 4th server moves less than 40% of keys, and that removing one only moves that server's keys.</p>`,
  ex: [['r.add("a"); r.get("k")', '"a"']], start: 'import bisect, hashlib\n\nclass Ring:\n    ', nudge: [],
  sol: `import bisect, hashlib\n\ndef _h(text):\n    return int(hashlib.md5(text.encode()).hexdigest(), 16)\n\nclass Ring:\n    def __init__(self, replicas=100):\n        self.replicas = replicas\n        self.points = []\n        self.owner = {}\n\n    def add(self, server):\n        for i in range(self.replicas):\n            p = _h(server + "#" + str(i))\n            self.owner[p] = server\n            bisect.insort(self.points, p)\n\n    def remove(self, server):\n        for i in range(self.replicas):\n            p = _h(server + "#" + str(i))\n            del self.owner[p]\n            self.points.remove(p)\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if not self.points:\n            return None\n        i = bisect.bisect_left(self.points, _h(key))\n        return self.owner[self.points[i % len(self.points)]]`, big: 'O(log n) per get', idea: 'Servers and keys hash onto one ring. Replicas smooth out the load.',
  tests: (() => { const s = 'r = Ring()\nfor x in "abc":\n    r.add(x)\nkeys = ["key" + str(i) for i in range(3000)]\n'; return [['Ring().get("k")', 'None'], ['r.get("k")', '"a"', 'r = Ring()\nr.add("a")\n'], ['r.get("k")', 'None', 'r = Ring()\nr.add("a")\nr.remove("a")\n'], ['set(r.get(k) for k in keys)', 'set("abc")', s], ['min(c.values()) > 500', 'True', s + 'import collections\nc = collections.Counter(r.get(k) for k in keys)\n'], ['r.get("same") == r.get("same")', 'True', s], ['moved < 0.4 * len(keys)', 'True', s + 'old = {k: r.get(k) for k in keys}\nr.add("d")\nmoved = sum(old[k] != r.get(k) for k in keys)\n'], ['all(r.get(k) == "d" for k in keys if old[k] != r.get(k))', 'True', s + 'old = {k: r.get(k) for k in keys}\nr.add("d")\n'], ['all(old[k] == r.get(k) for k in keys if old[k] != "c")', 'True', s + 'old = {k: r.get(k) for k in keys}\nr.remove("c")\n']]; })() }
});
